WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.280
Part 2. You will hear a podcast about a large business exhibition which is held every year

00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:14.560
in London. First, you have some time to look at questions 11 to 14.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:49.279
Hello, and welcome to this podcast telling you about our annual business exhibition.

00:00:49.279 --> 00:00:56.880
This year, as always, it will be full of the very best speakers, features and innovations.

00:00:56.880 --> 00:01:02.279
Let me start by reading some feedback from James Craig, who has exhibited with us for

00:01:02.279 --> 00:01:09.940
the past two years. James says, I'm director of a company called TalkCon, which is an office

00:01:09.940 --> 00:01:16.400
phone system. The first year we exhibited, we had a stand in the business village where

00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:22.440
we made a lot of useful contacts. We also used the breakout area where people could

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:28.839
sit down and relax and try out our products, and due to popular request, we used that

00:01:28.839 --> 00:01:35.919
again last year. Last year, we also sponsored the innovative business connection zone, where

00:01:35.919 --> 00:01:41.480
people could leave their contact details on a board to contact other companies, and that

00:01:41.480 --> 00:01:48.440
also effectively raised our corporate profile. We're hoping that in the coming exhibition,

00:01:48.440 --> 00:01:53.160
we'll also have a presence in the digital marketing centre, which is clearly a key area

00:01:53.160 --> 00:02:01.800
for our company, and also maybe have our own TalkCon zone at some stage. The exhibitions

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:08.440
open from 8am to 11pm, and there's a range of special events for you to enjoy in the

00:02:08.440 --> 00:02:15.000
evening, as well as a unique chance to publicise your company. Businesses who exhibit with

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:22.399
us can claim discounts on a number of popular brands, including major high street fashion

00:02:22.399 --> 00:02:30.720
and jewellery outlets, as well as grocery chains. Discounts available range from 3.5%

00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:38.679
up to an amazing 15%, so you can save a lot of money. In fact, the average saving made

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:45.720
by each exhibitor came to over £400. The scheme is available to every member of your

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:52.720
organisation, so this is a benefit that really does have something for you all.

00:03:08.679 --> 00:03:15.720
Now listen, and answer questions 15 to 20.

00:03:38.720 --> 00:03:44.720
Now let me tell you about some of our keynote speakers for this year's exhibition.

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:51.720
Jim Clowery started off selling vegetables from a small plot, then opened a small cafe.

00:03:51.720 --> 00:03:57.720
Rather to his surprise, this became a bit of a sensation, and in a remarkably short

00:03:57.720 --> 00:04:05.720
time, he had opened an amazing 76 restaurants worldwide. He'll be telling you how it all

00:04:05.759 --> 00:04:12.759
happened, and how it changed his life. David France will be giving an inspirational talk

00:04:12.759 --> 00:04:18.760
about how, as a business-minded teenager, he managed to set up his own company, and

00:04:18.760 --> 00:04:24.760
even to be the first person under 18 to get a business bank account. David's company

00:04:24.760 --> 00:04:30.760
has gone from strength to strength, but he isn't just interested in making money. He

00:04:30.799 --> 00:04:36.799
also makes regular donations to organisations, helping those in need, both in the UK and

00:04:36.799 --> 00:04:43.799
elsewhere in the world. Oliver Stanton was born and educated in the UK, and then went

00:04:43.799 --> 00:04:49.799
to Malaysia, where he set up a rubber wood furniture business. He then built log houses

00:04:49.799 --> 00:04:56.799
in the mountains in Japan, before moving to Hong Kong to set up a digital marketing business.

00:04:56.839 --> 00:05:03.839
After that, he moved back to the UK with his wife Sai Pin, and together they opened a Thai

00:05:03.839 --> 00:05:09.839
cafe. He'll be telling you about what he discovered during his travels around the world, about

00:05:09.839 --> 00:05:16.839
business practices in different cultures. Francesca Heptonstall is a broadcaster and

00:05:16.839 --> 00:05:22.839
businesswoman who is known for her down-to-earth attitude. After winning a top job at a TV

00:05:22.880 --> 00:05:29.880
contest, she launched a number of online deal sites. Her main business interest is in technology,

00:05:30.880 --> 00:05:36.880
but she's also passionate about helping people gain employment, and organises an annual jobs

00:05:36.880 --> 00:05:43.880
fair, so she'll be concentrating on this in her talk. Salman Khan is the chief executive

00:05:43.880 --> 00:05:49.880
of QBF Enterprises, and has turned around this business from a major publishing business

00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:55.920
to one of the UK's largest digital marketing service providers. He'll be talking about

00:05:55.920 --> 00:06:02.920
the need for flexibility to cope with new directions in today's business world.

00:06:02.920 --> 00:06:09.920
Finally, Annie Craven is a consultant and coach who works with people to create lasting

00:06:09.920 --> 00:06:16.920
changes in their business and life. Her session will offer an interactive look at different

00:06:16.959 --> 00:06:22.959
obstacles in people's own lives, not just in business. She also looks at how you can

00:06:22.959 --> 00:06:29.959
overcome them, whether you're starting out in business or at the top of your game.

00:06:34.959 --> 00:06:41.959
That is the end of part two. You now have 30 seconds to check your answers to part two.

